{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1426365","title":"Sulfammox reactor sludge and biofilm metagenomes, metatranscriptomes, and 16S amplicons","description":"This study investigated the microbial mechanisms underlying ammonia oxidation in a sulfate-reducing anaerobic ammonium oxidation (sulfammox) system. A laboratory-scale upflow anaerobic sludge blanket (UASB) reactor was operated for 207 days under inorganic conditions with varying influent sulfate concentrations (30-1000 mg/L), with or without nitrite supplementation, and with or without recirculation. To elucidate the metabolic pathways involved, we performed multi-omics analyses including 16S rRNA gene amplicon sequencing, metagenomics, and metatranscriptomics on reactor sludge samples collected at nine time points and recirculation line biofilm samples collected at four time points. The dataset revealed that ammonia oxidation was driven by cryptic oxygen permeation through silicone tubing, enriching nitrifying bacteria (Nitrosomonas) and anammox bacteria (Candidatus_Brocadia), while dissimilatory sulfate reduction genes remained negligible. These findings overturn the long-standing sulfammox hypothesis and demonstrate that the observed nitrogen removal is actually mediated by oxygen-driven partial nitritation-anammox, not by direct coupling to sulfate reduction. The data provide fundamental insights into nitrogen-sulfur interactions in engineered systems and support the development of passive oxygen delivery strategies for energy-efficient wastewater treatment.","organism":"bioreactor sludge metagenome","data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"RCEES","submission_date":"2026/02/20 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","location_resolution","country_iso2","place_id","location_status","location_m